      <h2>Insuring the baby, and the paperwork</h2>
      <p>In Germany, a newborn is automatically covered for free under a parent's public plan through family co-insurance, <strong>Familienversicherung</strong>: no extra premium, for the first year or the next eighteen. In the US, the baby must be added to a plan, usually within thirty days, and moving to family coverage can add two hundred to five hundred dollars a month in premiums, roughly twenty-five hundred to six thousand dollars over the first year. It's one of the quietest line items and one of the most expensive.</p>

      <p>Paperwork is the rare place the costs look almost identical. In Germany, a birth is registered at the local registry office, the Standesamt, within a week, and certified copies of the birth certificate run around twelve euros. In the US, the hospital files the record, the Social Security number is free, and certified copies run roughly fifteen to thirty-five dollars. Call it a draw. Baby gear is close too: diapers run sixty to eighty a month, and all-in gear and supplies land somewhere between two and five thousand in either currency, with secondhand culture softening it on both sides.</p>

